1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,760
The same game can feel open in

2
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:07,740
one view and cramped in another.

3
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:12,840
Even with identical graphics, what we fit on
the screen changes how the game plays.

4
00:00:13,140 --> 00:00:15,580
We are not seeing the entire game world,

5
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:17,720
only the part that fits inside our monitor.

6
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:22,900
How much of our real view the screen occupies
depends on its size and our viewing distance.

7
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:26,940
How much scenery the game camera fits into
that screen is a different question.

8
00:00:27,180 --> 00:00:32,280
Today is not about cars. It is about the frame
around a game: aspect ratio,

9
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:35,600
field of view, and the size of important
objects on screen.

10
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:39,420
First, aspect ratio. Numbers like 16:9 and
21:9 describe

11
00:00:39,420 --> 00:00:43,520
the relationship between a screen's width and
height.

12
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:47,980
A wider screen does not reveal more of the
world in every game.

13
00:00:48,060 --> 00:00:50,940
If the vertical field of view stays fixed as
the screen widens,

14
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:53,500
more space can become visible on either side.

15
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:56,600
Other games may enlarge and crop the image

16
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:59,040
at the top and bottom, or add black bars.

17
00:00:59,180 --> 00:01:02,380
Our comparison keeps the vertical field of
view fixed.

18
00:01:02,380 --> 00:01:06,740
Widening the frame reveals the sides; it does
not stretch the objects horizontally.

19
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:11,760
When changing aspect ratio, decide what to
reveal and what to preserve.

20
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:15,280
Check that the interface does not move off
screen, too.

21
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:19,120
Could we solve this by widening the camera's
viewing angle?

22
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:23,000
This is called field of view, or FOV.

23
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:26,080
From the same position, fitting more scenery
on the

24
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:28,420
same screen lets us see more of our
surroundings.

25
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:32,700
But with a perspective camera, the same object
in the center appears smaller.

26
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:35,720
Narrowing the view makes the object ahead
larger,

27
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:38,360
but it is easier to miss something approaching
from the side.

28
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:41,200
Seeing the sides clearly is little help if the
car ahead becomes a speck.

29
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:44,340
Staring at that car and missing the one beside
us is not ideal either.

30
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:47,160
Reading the surroundings and distinguishing
small

31
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:49,980
objects: the right balance depends on the
game.

32
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:53,960
In shooters, this trade-off is even more
immediate.

33
00:01:54,180 --> 00:01:59,200
You may buy a big monitor, yet the enemy you
need to hit is still a tiny dot on screen.

34
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:01,940
If the fun is finding distant enemies and
aiming precisely,

35
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:04,900
small targets can be an intentional challenge.

36
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:08,540
If the fun is dodging nearby attacks and
reading an enemy's movements,

37
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:11,380
those movements need to be large enough to
read.

38
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:14,900
Enlarging enemies is not the only option. You
can shorten combat distances

39
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:18,300
or use colors and silhouettes that stand out
from the background.

40
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:20,700
The point is not simply to make things bigger.

41
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:24,220
First decide what the player should notice and
judge.

42
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:28,700
Even a wide screen can leave little room for
the information needed to make decisions.

43
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:31,880
Think of a racing cockpit, a large weapon in a
shooter,

44
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:34,200
or an instruction panel filling the screen.

45
00:02:34,540 --> 00:02:37,600
These can add atmosphere. But if they hide the
next corner

46
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:40,220
or an enemy's movement, the readable space
shrinks.

47
00:02:40,420 --> 00:02:44,300
We came here to drive, not attend a dashboard
appreciation exhibition.

48
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:49,340
That does not mean removing everything. Keep
essential information such as speed

49
00:02:49,340 --> 00:02:52,340
and health, and adjust the size and placement
of decoration and instructions.

50
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:54,600
What matters is not how full the screen looks,

51
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:58,620
but how quickly players can find the clues
they need.

52
00:02:59,360 --> 00:03:01,760
Now pause a frame of your own game.

53
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:05,540
Where is the object the player needs to see,
and how large does it appear?

54
00:03:05,740 --> 00:03:10,060
Are the character, weapon, or instruction
panels hiding clues to the next action?

55
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:12,860
If information is too small to read, try
changing size or contrast.

56
00:03:13,220 --> 00:03:16,240
If players miss their surroundings, try
changing the camera or layout.

57
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:19,500
Do not test only on your large development
monitor.

58
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:23,100
Test at the screen sizes and viewing distances
people will actually use.

59
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:26,500
Aspect ratio is the shape of the window. FOV
is the range it shows.

60
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:31,520
Screen occupancy is how much space an object
takes up. They are related, but not the same.

61
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:34,700
A good screen does not make everything large.

62
00:03:35,140 --> 00:03:38,680
It makes the things we need to see, judge,

63
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:42,420
and act on clear. Even a small window can hold
a great game.
